    "top_frame": "They never trailed. They led after the first inning and never looked back.",
    "what_this_means": "Bryce Eldridge carried the offensive load, going 4-for-5 with 4 RBIs to anchor a 7-2 win over Arizona. The bullpen held after Yunior Marte's early exit, with Carson Seymour and Dylan Smith combining for three scoreless innings in the back half. The Giants converted 6 of 16 RISP situations and scored in four separate innings, building the lead methodically rather than explosively. This is the kind of game where the structure worked: starter held the line long enough, relievers cleaned up, and enough contact reached enough bases to matter. That's what a managed win looks like for a team still 25.5 games back.",
    "what_to_watch": "Eldridge's performance mattered not because the Giants dominated but because he was the only separator in a relatively even offensive day. Nine hits total, eight strikeouts. The lineup was there, but one player made broad contact into actual runs. This continues the season-long inconsistency: on some nights, someone finds the middle of the order and the game breaks open; on others, the same situation produces nothing. The win matters less than the pattern it reveals. They beat Arizona because they didn't need much, and Eldridge made what little they had count. Against Atlanta's Elder tomorrow, they'll need the same formula. Enough contact, one player converting it, a start that holds.",
